 @arcticowl1091  applications are native to win32, not windows. Windows then implements the win32 api allowing applications to be made that don't have to depend on specific windows versions. All wine does is implement the win32 api itself, just in Linux instead of windows. It is literally a native implementation of win32.

If what you are claiming is true then any time windows updated or got a new version then those applications would no longer run on them natively. This is just not the case. Even when microsoft updates their implementation of win32 they keep the interface the same so that applications don't break.

Either games made for windows 10 are not natively running on windows 11 and wine is also not running natively or games made for windows 10 do run natively on windows 11 and the same holds true when ran on wine.

 @HakanBayndr  how is anything running in Wine Linux native? If the binary doesn't start with the letters ELF it is NOT Linux native! You use a hex dumping utility to see that data. This is what Linux native is supposed to look like
$  hexdump -C `which bash` | head
00000000  7f 45 4c 46 02 01 01 00  00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00  |.ELF............|

 @HakanBayndr  I have a .NET program that runs in Linux. But it isn't Linux native either. It's a MZ binary. $  file Builder.exe
Builder.exe: PE32+ executable (GUI) x86-64 Mono/.Net assembly, for MS Windows
So not Linux native. It still works good though. That's a Doom editor. Ultimate Doom Builder. Now the Doom engine I run it's native Linux.

